# Chapter 7 · Roots of the Movement in South Asia and the Pakistan Movement

> Pakistan's women's movement did not begin in 1981 or in 2018. It grew from **nineteenth-century social reform**, from **Muslim women's education**, and from women's part in the **Pakistan Movement** itself. A 2026 MCQ asked who led the reform movement that advocated women's education and opposed *sati* (answer: **Raja Ram Mohan Roy**).

## 7.1  Social reform in nineteenth-century India

Reformers — mostly men at first — campaigned against customs that harmed women.

::: table | Nineteenth-century reforms affecting women in British India.
@widths 1.6,5,7.4
Year | Reform | Who and what
==1829== | Bengal Sati Regulation | **Raja Ram Mohan Roy**'s campaign against *sati* (widow burning); banned under Governor-General Bentinck
==1856== | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act | **Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar**'s campaign for widows' right to remarry
==1882== | *Stri Purush Tulana* | **Tarabai Shinde**'s attack on double standards
==1891== | Age of Consent Act | Raised the age of consent for girls from 10 to 12
==1929== | Child Marriage Restraint Act ("Sarda Act") | Set minimum marriage ages (14 for girls, later raised); still the base law in parts of Pakistan until provincial reforms
==1939== | Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act | Gave Muslim women grounds to seek divorce through the courts
:::

## 7.2  Muslim women's education and reform

- **Sir Syed Ahmad Khan** (Aligarh, 1875) focused on Muslim men's modern education and was cautious about girls' schooling; others pushed further.
- **Deputy Nazir Ahmad**'s novel *Mirat-ul-Uroos* (==1869==) promoted girls' education within the home.
- **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** published *Huquq-un-Niswan* (==1898==) in Lahore, arguing from the Qur'an for women's rights; with his wife **Muhammadi Begum** he ran the women's journal *Tahzib-e-Niswan* (from ==1898==).
- **Sheikh Abdullah** ("Papa Mian") and **Wahid Jahan Begum** founded a girls' school at **Aligarh** in ==1906==, later Aligarh Women's College.
- **Begum Rokeya Sakhawat Hossain** wrote *Sultana's Dream* (==1905==) and founded a girls' school in Calcutta (==1911==).
- **Sultan Jahan Begum**, ruler of Bhopal, supported Muslim women's education and associations.
- The **All-India Muslim Ladies' Conference** (*Anjuman-e-Khawateen-e-Islam*), from ==1914==, called for education and spoke against polygamy and purdah abuses.
- The **Shariat Application Act of 1937** applied Islamic personal law to Indian Muslims; women activists supported it partly because it gave women inheritance rights that custom denied.

::: simple
Early Muslim reformers wanted women to be educated **as good Muslim wives and mothers**. This "reform within tradition" shaped Pakistani feminism: it often argues from Islam and asks for education, inheritance and family-law rights first.
:::

## 7.3  Women in the Pakistan Movement

- The **All-India Muslim League** formed a **Central Women's Sub-Committee** at its Patna session in ==1938==, to bring women into the movement.
- **Fatima Jinnah**, the Quaid's sister, travelled with him and became the symbol of Muslim women's political role — later called ***Madar-e-Millat*** ("Mother of the Nation").
- **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Shaista Suhrawardy Ikramullah** became the **first two women members** of Pakistan's Constituent Assembly (==1947==).
- **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** organised women's volunteer services.
- During the civil disobedience campaign in Punjab in **February 1947**, a teenage student, **Fatima Sughra**, replaced the Union Jack with the Muslim League flag on the Punjab Civil Secretariat in Lahore.
- Women organised **processions, relief work** and **picketing**, and many were arrested.

::: quote Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah | Aligarh, 10 March 1944
"No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you. We are victims of evil customs. It is a crime against humanity that our women are shut up within the four walls of the houses as prisoners."
-> The line most quoted by Pakistani feminists of every school.
:::

## 7.3a  Women leaders of the Pakistan Movement

::: table | Women of the Pakistan Movement you should be able to name.
@widths 4.2,9.8
Leader | Role
Fatima Jinnah (1893–1967) | Dentist; the Quaid's constant companion; led women's work in the Muslim League; *Madar-e-Millat*; presidential candidate in 1965
Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz (1896–1979) | The only Muslim woman delegate at the **Round Table Conferences** (1930–32); member of the first Constituent Assembly
Begum Shaista Ikramullah (1915–2000) | First Muslim woman to earn a PhD from the University of London; member of the first Constituent Assembly; later a diplomat
Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan (1905–1990) | Economist; organised women volunteers; founded APWA (1949); later Governor of Sindh
Lady Abdullah Haroon | Led women's Muslim League work in Sindh
Begum Salma Tassaduq Hussain | Led the Punjab women's Muslim League; arrested during the 1947 civil disobedience
Begum Viqar-un-Nisa Noon (1920–2000) | Muslim League worker in the 1947 Punjab agitation; later active in social welfare
Fatima Sughra (1931–2017) | As a teenager replaced the Union Jack with the Muslim League flag on the Punjab Civil Secretariat (1947)
:::

These women came mostly from educated, prominent families. Their activism was framed as **service to the nation and the Muslim community**, which made it respectable — and set the tone for APWA's welfare approach after 1947.

## 7.4  Partition and the refugee crisis (1947)

Partition was the first great test. Millions of refugees crossed the new borders; tens of thousands of women were **abducted, raped or killed** on both sides. Women volunteers, led by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**, ran camps, hospitals and recovery work. The **Women's Voluntary Service** and the **Women's National Guard** grew out of this effort, and in ==1949== they led to **APWA** (Chapter 8).

::: fig flow | From reform to movement: the roots of Pakistan's women's movement.
@per 3
Social reform (1829–1891): sati, widow remarriage, age of consent
Muslim reform (1869–1914): education, journals, ladies' conferences
Legal gains (1937–1939): Shariat Act, Muslim divorce law
The Pakistan Movement (1938–1947): women's committees, marches
Partition relief (1947): camps, hospitals, volunteers
APWA (1949): the first national women's organisation
:::

## 7.4a  The first test after independence: women's inheritance (1948)

One of the first campaigns in the new state was for women's right to **inherit agricultural land**. Customary law in Punjab often denied daughters and widows their Islamic share. Women legislators, including **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Salma Tassaduq Hussain**, pressed for the **West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act 1948**, and women demonstrated outside the Punjab Assembly in its support. The Act applied Islamic inheritance rules, including to agricultural land. It showed a pattern that would recur: women's rights won by **appealing to Islam against custom**.

## 7.5  What the roots explain

1. **Reform from within Islam.** From Mumtaz Ali to today's Islamic feminists, many Pakistani activists argue for women's rights in Islamic terms.
2. **Education first.** Girls' schooling has been the most accepted demand since the nineteenth century.
3. **Elite leadership.** The early movement was led by educated women of prominent families — a pattern that continued into APWA and WAF.
4. **Nationalism.** Women's rights were tied to building the nation, which gave them legitimacy but also made them depend on the state's goodwill.
5. **The vote came with independence.** Unlike the West, Pakistani women did not need a first-wave suffrage campaign; universal adult franchise came with the new state.

::: pakistan | Why this chapter matters in the exam
When you "trace the evolution" of the movement (2022, 2026) or answer the "Western import" objection (2019, 2023, 2024), start here. Pakistan's movement has **local roots** older than the second wave: social reform, Muslim reformist writing, and women's role in creating the country.
:::
